WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.113 --> 00:00:13.981
At sunset Dr.

2
00:00:14.001 --> 00:00:16.423
Losberne entered the parlor, where Oliver and Mrs.

3
00:00:16.483 --> 00:00:18.004
Maylie had been sitting for hours.

4
00:00:18.024 --> 00:00:19.986
"Tell me at once!"

5
00:00:20.426 --> 00:00:20.906
cried Mrs.

6
00:00:20.966 --> 00:00:22.268
Maylie, rising from the couch.

7
00:00:22.288 --> 00:00:24.249
"Is Rose dead?"

8
00:00:24.269 --> 00:00:27.472
"Compose yourself, dear ma'am," said the doctor.

9
00:00:27.492 --> 00:00:32.156
"Rose has survived, and she will live to bless us all for years to come."

10
00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:35.181
The good news was almost too much to bear.

11
00:00:36.201 --> 00:00:38.142
Mrs. Maylie sank with relief into Dr.

12
00:00:38.182 --> 00:00:39.083
Losberne's arms.

13
00:00:40.203 --> 00:00:41.123
Oliver was stunned.

14
00:00:42.144 --> 00:00:45.845
He could not weep or speak or remain in that room for one moment longer.

15
00:00:46.906 --> 00:00:49.407
He went out for a long, rambling walk in the evening air.

16
00:00:50.527 --> 00:00:52.528
At last he burst into tears of relief.

17
00:00:53.669 --> 00:00:56.150
Wiping his eyes, he began to gather flowers for Rose.

18
00:00:57.210 --> 00:01:01.352
The night was closing in as he headed homeward, his arms laden with a huge bouquet.

19
00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:06.661
As he walked along the road, he heard the sound of a carriage approaching from behind.

20
00:01:07.741 --> 00:01:10.642
It dashed past him, and he caught a glimpse of a familiar face.

21
00:01:11.702 --> 00:01:12.463
Mr. Giles!

22
00:01:13.523 --> 00:01:16.384
The carriage came to a halt, and the butler stuck his head out the window.

23
00:01:16.404 --> 00:01:18.724
"Oliver!" cried Mr.

24
00:01:18.744 --> 00:01:21.245
Giles. "How is Miss Rose?"

25
00:01:22.345 --> 00:01:23.546
Oliver ran up to the coach.

26
00:01:24.646 --> 00:01:26.006
A young gentleman pushed Mr.

27
00:01:26.026 --> 00:01:27.767
Giles aside and addressed Oliver.

28
00:01:27.787 --> 00:01:29.848
"Is she better or worse?"

29
00:01:29.868 --> 00:01:33.157
"Better. Much better, sir!"

30
00:01:33.517 --> 00:01:34.077
said Oliver.

31
00:01:34.097 --> 00:01:35.818
"Are you sure?"

32
00:01:36.839 --> 00:01:39.380
The gentleman jumped out of the carriage and took Oliver by the arm.

33
00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:41.581
"I'm quite sure, sir.

34
00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:44.643
Dr. Losberne says that she's out of danger."

35
00:01:45.783 --> 00:01:48.785
The gentleman turned his face away and remained silent for some moments.

36
00:01:50.586 --> 00:01:52.587
Oliver thought he heard the man sobbing while Mr.

37
00:01:52.627 --> 00:01:57.370
Giles sat on the carriage steps, wiping his eyes with a blue handkerchief dotted with white spots.

38
00:01:57.390 --> 00:02:01.312
"You go ahead to my mother's house, Giles," said the young man.

39
00:02:02.452 --> 00:02:04.633
"I'd rather walk to gain a little time before I see her."

40
00:02:04.653 --> 00:02:06.754
"I beg your pardon, Mr.

41
00:02:06.774 --> 00:02:08.455
Harry, but I'd like to walk with you."

42
00:02:09.516 --> 00:02:12.297
Mr. Giles gave his face one final wipe with the handkerchief.

43
00:02:12.317 --> 00:02:15.719
"It wouldn't be proper for the staff to see me in this state.

44
00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:19.021
I'd never have any authority over them if they did."

45
00:02:20.002 --> 00:02:21.062
Harry Maylie smiled.

46
00:02:21.082 --> 00:02:23.043
"You may do as you like."

47
00:02:24.044 --> 00:02:26.265
So the three of them walked slowly toward the cottage.

48
00:02:27.266 --> 00:02:30.007
Mrs. Maylie was anxiously waiting for her son when he arrived.

49
00:02:31.532 --> 00:02:33.193
"Mother!" said the young man as they embraced.

50
00:02:33.213 --> 00:02:35.555
"Why didn't you write to me sooner?"

51
00:02:35.575 --> 00:02:37.717
"I did.

52
00:02:38.718 --> 00:02:41.040
But then I decided not to send the letter until Dr.

53
00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:42.341
Losberne had seen Rose."

54
00:02:42.361 --> 00:02:44.523
"But what if she had died?"

55
00:02:44.863 --> 00:02:45.523
chided Harry.

56
00:02:45.544 --> 00:02:47.545
"You know I love her!"

57
00:02:47.565 --> 00:02:51.328
"I know that she deserves the best love that a man can offer.

58
00:02:52.349 --> 00:02:54.931
And now I must say something that it pains me to say."

59
00:02:55.992 --> 00:02:56.993
Mrs. Maylie paused.

60
00:02:57.013 --> 00:03:00.035
"Sometimes a young man's love doesn't last."

61
00:03:01.432 --> 00:03:02.593
"That is unkind, Mother."

62
00:03:02.613 --> 00:03:05.535
"Rose has a stain upon her name, Harry.

63
00:03:06.575 --> 00:03:07.496
She's illegitimate.

64
00:03:08.496 --> 00:03:11.218
And you're ambitious, which means people will sneer at her."

65
00:03:12.238 --> 00:03:14.340
Mrs. Maylie fixed her eyes on her son's face.

66
00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:18.282
"If you marry her, you may live to regret your decision.

67
00:03:19.423 --> 00:03:21.484
And seeing your regret will only hurt Rose."

68
00:03:21.504 --> 00:03:23.985
"Are you going to oppose my marriage?"

69
00:03:24.386 --> 00:03:24.926
asked Harry.

70
00:03:24.946 --> 00:03:32.618
"I won't, but I'd like you to consider—" "I have considered these things for years and years," Harry

71
00:03:32.638 --> 00:03:33.379
said impatiently.

72
00:03:33.399 --> 00:03:36.801
"My feelings remain unchanged, and they always will.

73
00:03:37.762 --> 00:03:39.403
There is no point in remaining silent.

74
00:03:40.423 --> 00:03:42.445
Before I leave this place, Rose will hear me."

75
00:03:42.465 --> 00:03:45.387
"Yes, she will hear you," Mrs.

76
00:03:45.447 --> 00:03:46.487
Maylie echoed flatly.

77
00:03:46.507 --> 00:03:50.430
"You sound as if she will hear my offer of marriage but not accept me.

78
00:03:51.451 --> 00:03:52.932
Has she fallen in love with someone else?"

79
00:03:53.312 --> 00:03:53.952
Harry demanded.

80
00:03:53.973 --> 00:03:55.934
"No indeed.

81
00:03:56.935 --> 00:03:58.816
You have a strong hold on her affections.

82
00:03:59.884 --> 00:04:01.025
But think of her history.

83
00:04:02.105 --> 00:04:04.886
Her awareness of her situation may influence her decision.

84
00:04:05.887 --> 00:04:09.068
Devoted as she is to us, she can be self-sacrificing."

85
00:04:10.108 --> 00:04:10.829
"What do you mean?"

86
00:04:10.849 --> 00:04:14.450
"I leave that to you to discover," replied Mrs.

87
00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:17.111
Maylie. "I must go back to her now."

88
00:04:17.131 --> 00:04:19.212
"You will tell her I am here?"

89
00:04:19.592 --> 00:04:20.112
said Harry.

90
00:04:20.132 --> 00:04:22.393
"Of course."

91
00:04:22.413 --> 00:04:25.855
"And say how anxious I have been and how I long to see her?

92
00:04:26.895 --> 00:04:28.256
You will not refuse to do this, Mother?"

93
00:04:29.452 --> 00:04:31.073
"No, I will tell her everything."

94
00:04:32.134 --> 00:04:34.035
Pressing her son's hand affectionately, Mrs.

95
00:04:34.075 --> 00:04:34.895
Maylie left the room.

96
00:04:35.976 --> 00:04:38.157
Harry was soon able to see Rose for himself.

97
00:04:39.297 --> 00:04:41.599
But he knew that now was not the time to talk of marriage.

98
00:04:42.619 --> 00:04:44.240
That would have to wait until she was better.

99
00:04:45.341 --> 00:04:48.562
In the meantime he went out every morning with Oliver to pick flowers for her.

100
00:04:49.623 --> 00:04:52.424
The days flew by, and Rose was rapidly recovering.

101
00:04:53.505 --> 00:04:56.427
She was still confined to her bed, so there were no evening walks.

102
00:04:57.527 --> 00:04:58.748
But Oliver was not idle.

103
00:04:59.796 --> 00:05:03.078
He applied himself to his studies and surprised himself with how much he learned.

104
00:05:04.118 --> 00:05:07.260
The little room where he studied was on the ground floor at the back of the house.

105
00:05:08.921 --> 00:05:12.883
The window overlooked a garden, and beyond that lay fine meadowland and woods.

106
00:05:14.184 --> 00:05:17.045
One warm evening at twilight, Oliver sat reading in this room.

107
00:05:18.046 --> 00:05:21.168
He had been poring over his books for some time and began to grow drowsy.

108
00:05:22.108 --> 00:05:23.389
Gradually he fell asleep.

109
00:05:24.329 --> 00:05:28.051
There is a kind of sleep in which the person is still conscious of sounds and things going

110
00:05:28.132 --> 00:05:28.732
on around him.

111
00:05:29.664 --> 00:05:31.165
That is the state that Oliver was in.

112
00:05:32.186 --> 00:05:35.688
He knew perfectly well that he was in his own little room and that his books were lying

113
00:05:35.728 --> 00:05:36.729
on the table before him.

114
00:05:37.790 --> 00:05:38.810
And yet he was asleep.

115
00:05:39.951 --> 00:05:42.973
Suddenly the scene changed, and the air became dank and heavy.

116
00:05:44.034 --> 00:05:46.976
He thought, with a shudder of terror, that he was in Fagin's house again.

117
00:05:48.016 --> 00:05:52.679
The hideous old man sat in his usual corner of the kitchen, pointing at Oliver and whispering to

118
00:05:52.719 --> 00:05:53.260
another man.

119
00:05:54.421 --> 00:05:57.142
Oliver thought he heard Fagin say, "Hush, my dear!

120
00:05:58.143 --> 00:05:59.264
It's the boy, sure enough.

121
00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:00.861
Come away."

122
00:06:00.881 --> 00:06:03.463
"That boy!"

123
00:06:03.763 --> 00:06:04.964
the other man seemed to answer.

124
00:06:04.984 --> 00:06:07.606
"I'd recognize him anywhere."

125
00:06:08.567 --> 00:06:11.970
The man seemed to say this with such dreadful hatred that Oliver woke up.

126
00:06:12.991 --> 00:06:13.751
He couldn't move.

127
00:06:14.752 --> 00:06:18.576
He couldn't speak because there—right there at the window—stood Fagin!

128
00:06:19.697 --> 00:06:22.339
Fagin was so close that Oliver could have almost touched him.

129
00:06:23.420 --> 00:06:29.048
And standing beside him, white with fear or rage or both, was the man from the inn—the angry

130
00:06:29.088 --> 00:06:31.089
man in the dark cloak who had threatened Oliver!

131
00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:33.991
A red scar marred the man's neck.

132
00:06:35.032 --> 00:06:38.313
The two men were at the window for only an instant, and then they were gone.

133
00:06:39.354 --> 00:06:42.116
But they had recognized Oliver, and he had recognized them.

134
00:06:43.256 --> 00:06:45.137
Oliver stood transfixed for a moment.

135
00:06:46.258 --> 00:06:50.000
Then, leaping from the window into the garden, he called loudly for help.
